                              %mo ⊣
                            %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dashv
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sharp+modality"} sharp modality
          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#properties"} Properties
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#RelationToMonads"} Relation to monads
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#relation_to_endoadjunctions"} Relation to endo-adjunctions
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %ul
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#GeometricModality"} Geometric modality – Grothendieck topology
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#closure_modality"} Closure modality
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#necessity_and_possibility"} Necessity and possibility
                %li
                  %a{:href => "#cohesive_and_differential_modality"} Cohesive and differential modality
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Modal type theory
            is a flavor of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+formation+rules"} type formation rules
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
            which on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            reduces to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
          %p
            Following (
            %a{:href => "#Moggi91"}> Moggi91
            ,
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#BentonBiermanPaiva98"} Benton, Bierman &amp; de Paiva 1998
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BiermanDePaiva96"} Bierman &amp; de Paiva 1996
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#BiermanDePaiva96"} 2000
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Kobayashi97"} Kobayashi 1997
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} co-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            on its type system, representing the intended
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29"} monads in computer science
            embody a notion of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computation"} computation
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+type+theory"} computational type theory
            here (
            %a{:href => "#BentonBiermanPaiva98"}> Benton, Bierman &amp; de Paiva 1998
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FairtloughMendler02"} Fairtlough-Mendler 02
          %p
            According to (
            %a{:href => "#BentonBiermanPaiva98"}> Benton, Bierman &amp; de Paiva 1998, p. 1-2
            ) this matches well with the default interpretation of (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S4"}> S4
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            as being about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modality"} modality
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            of “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/possibility"}> possibility
            ”:
          %blockquote
            %p
              The starting point for Moggi’s work is an explicit semantic distinction between
              %em computations
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %em values
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              is an object which interprets the values of a particular type, then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(A)
              is the object which models computation of that type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              For a wide variety of notions of computation, the unary operator
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T(-)
              turns out to have the categorical structure of a
              %em strong monad
              on an underlying cartesian closed category of values.
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [...]
              On a purely intuitive level and particularly if one thinks about non-termination, there is certainly something appealing about the idea that a computation of type
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              represents the possibility of a value of type
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            When the underlying type theory is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            these modalities are a “higher” generalization of traditional modalities, with “higher” in the sense of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            given by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-monads"} (∞,1)-monads
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman"} Shulman 12
            %a{:href => "#RSS"} Rijke, Shulman, Spitters
            ) for definition of such
            = succeed "," do
              %em higher modalities
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subuniverse"} reflective subuniverse
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToMonads Relation to monads
          %p
            At least in many cases, modalities in type theory are identified with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads+%28in+computer+science%29"} monads
            or comonads on the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universe"} universe
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
          %p
            See for instance (
            %a{:href => "#BentonBiermanPaiva98"}> Benton, Bierman &amp; de Paiva 1998, section 3.2
            ), (
            %a{:href => "#Kobayashi"}> Kobayashi
            ), (
            %a{:href => "#GabbayNanevski"}> Gabbay-Nanevski, section 8
            ), (
            %a{:href => "#Gaubault-LarrecqGoubault"}> Gaubault-Larrecq, Goubault, section 5.1
            ), (
            %a{:href => "#ParkHarper"}> Park-Harper, section 2.6
            ), (
            %a{:href => "#Shulman"}> Shulman
            ) as examples of the first, and (
            %a{:href => "#Moggi"}> Moggi, def. 4.7
            ), (
            %a{:href => "#AwodeyBirkedal"}> Awodey-Birkedal, section 4.2
            ) as examples of the second.
          %h3#relation_to_endoadjunctions Relation to endo-adjunctions
          %p
            An endo-adjunction on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lex+category"} lex category
            gives rise to a modal type theory satisfying the modal axiom K; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#dradjoint"} dependent right adjoint
            = succeed "-calculus." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#GeometricModality Geometric modality – Grothendieck topology
          %p
            As a special case of the
            %a{:href => "#RelationToMonads"} modalities-are-monads
            relation, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/site"} site
            underlying a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presheaf+topos"} presheaf topos
            is equivalently encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheafification"} sheafification
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad"} monad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi PSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi PSh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PSh(C) \to Sh(C) \hookrightarrow PSh(C)
            induced by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaf+topos"} sheaf topos
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+embedding"} geometric embedding
          %p
            When restricted to act on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated+object"} (-1)-truncated objects
            (i.e.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subterminal+objects"} subterminal objects
            or more generally
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+closure+operator"} universal closure operator
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+classifier"} subobject classifier
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+operator"} Lawvere-Tierney operator
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"} Bill Lawvere
          %blockquote
            %p
              A Grothendieck ‘topology’ appears most naturally as a modal operator of the nature ‘it is locally the case that’ (
              %a{:href => "#Lawvere"}> Lawvere
              ).
          %p Here, “It is locally the case that [X is /Euclidean/etc],” corresponds to, “X is locally metrizable/Euclidean/etc,” in the usual parlance.
          %p
            More discussion is in (
            %a{:href => "#Goldblatt"}> Goldblatt
            ), where this kind of modality is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em geometric modality
          %p
            For
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+toposes"} higher toposes
            = succeed "-truncated" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+modality"} higher modality
            in the internal
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %h3#closure_modality Closure modality
          %p
            The canonical monad on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+topos"} local topos
            gives rise to a closure modality on propositions in its internal language, as described in (
            %a{:href => "#AwodeyBirkedal"}> Awodey-Birkedal
            ).
          %h3#necessity_and_possibility Necessity and possibility
          %p
            See at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/necessity+and+possibility"} necessity and possibility
            the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/necessity+and+possibility#InFirstOrderLogicAndTypeTheory"} Possible worlds via dependent type theory
          %h3#cohesive_and_differential_modality Cohesive and differential modality
          %p
            By adding to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            three (higher) modalities that encode
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+object"} discrete types
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+object"} codiscrete types
            and hence implicitly a non-(co)-discrete notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesion"} cohesion
            one obtained
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            %em infinitesimal
            (co)discreteness yields
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+homotopy+type+theory"} differential homotopy type theory
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closure+operator"} closure operator
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"}> idempotent monad
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-monad"} idempotent (∞,1)-monad
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type"}> modal type
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti-modal+type"} anti-modal type
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+homotopy+type+theory"} modal homotopy type theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+logic"} adjoint logic
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reflective+subuniverse"} reflective subuniverse
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+type+theory"} geometric type theory
          %h2#References References
          %p
            The clear identification of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            on types with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            (see at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29"} monad (in computer science)
          %ul
            %li#Moggi91
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenio+Moggi"}> Eugenio Moggi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Notions of computation and monads. Information and Computation
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.disi.unige.it/person/MoggiE/ftp/ic91.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            This was observed (independently) to systematically yield constructive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            in (see also at
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+type+theory"} computational type theory
          %ul
            %li#BentonBiermanPaiva98
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nick+Benton"}> Nick Benton
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+Bierman"} Gavin Bierman
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valeria+de+Paiva"} Valeria de Paiva
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Computational Types from a Logical Perspective
                %strong 8
                2 (1998) 177-193 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/S0956796898002998"}> doi:10.1017/S0956796898002998
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.36.5778"} web
            %li#BiermanDePaiva96
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+M.+Bierman"}> Gavin M. Bierman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valeria+de+Paiva"} Valeria de Paiva
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Intuitionistic necessity revisited
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.researchgate.net/publication/2810611_Intuitionistic_Necessity_Revisited"} researchgate
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Bierman-dePaiva-NecessityRevisited.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
            %li#BiermanDePaiva00
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gavin+M.+Bierman"}> Gavin M. Bierman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valeria+de+Paiva"} Valeria de Paiva
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On an Intuitionistic Modal Logic
                %strong 65
                (2000) 383–416 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1005291931660"}> doi:10.1023/A:1005291931660
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.researchgate.net/profile/Valeria-De-Paiva/publication/226515897_On_An_Intuitionistic_Modal_Logic/links/00b4951ed416906ccc000000/On-An-Intuitionistic-Modal-Logic.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Kobayashi97
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Satoshi+Kobayashi"}> Satoshi Kobayashi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Monad as modality
                %strong 175
                1 (1997) 29-74 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/S0304-3975(96)00169-7"}> doi:10.1016/S0304-3975(96)00169-7
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#FairloughMendler
              %p
                M. Fairlough, Michael Mendler,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Propositional lax logic
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.uni-bamberg.de/fileadmin/uni/fakultaeten/wiai_professuren/grundlagen_informatik/papersMM/pll.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Awodey"}> Steve Awodey
                , p. 279 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Category theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780198568612.001.0001"} doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780198568612.001.0001
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://global.oup.com/ukhe/product/category-theory-9780199237180"} ISBN:9780199237180
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://englishonlineclub.com/pdf/Category%20Teory%20%5BEnglishOnlineClub.com%5D.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            See also the type-theoretic generalization of
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+logic"} adjoint logic
          %ul
            %li#LicataShulman
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dan+Licata"}> Dan Licata
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
              = succeed "," do
                %em Adjoint logic with a 2-category of modes
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://lfcs.info/lfcs-2016/"} Logical Foundations of Computer Science 2016
              (
              %a{:href => "http://dlicata.web.wesleyan.edu/pubs/ls15adjoint/ls15adjoint.pdf"}> pdf
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dlicata.web.wesleyan.edu/pubs/ls15adjoint/ls15adjoint-lfcs-slides.pdf"} slides
          %p
            The modal systems “CL” and “PLL” in (
            %a{:href => "#BentonBiermanPaiva"}> Benton, Bierman &amp; de Paiva 1998
            ) and (
            %a{:href => "#FairloughMendler"}> Fairlough-Mendler
            ), respectively, turn out to be equivalent to the geometric modality of
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Goldblatt"} Goldblatt
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Kobayashi97"} Kobayashi 1997
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/S4+modal+logic"} S4 modal logic
          %p
            Explicit mentioning of type theory equipped with such a monad as
            %em modal type theory
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/computational+type+theory"} computational type theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#FairtloughMendler02
              Matt Fairtlough, Michael Mendler,
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the Logical Content of Computational Type Theory: A Solution to Curry’s Problem
          %p
            Discussion of modal operators explicitly in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            (and with a brief mentioning of the relation to
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monad+%28in+computer+science%29"} monads
          %ul
            %li#NanevskiPfenningPientka05
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aleksandar+Nanevski"}> Aleksandar Nanevski
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Pfenning"} Frank Pfenning
                %em Contextual Modal Type Theory
                (2005) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "http://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/viewdoc/summary?doi=10.1.1.61.2356"}> web
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://wips.cs.umn.edu/slides/pientka.pdf"} slides
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valeria+de+Paiva"}> Valeria de Paiva
                , Eike Ritter,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fibrational Modal Type Theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1571066116300378"} doi:10.1016/j.entcs.2016.06.010
            %li
              %p
                Daniel Gratzer,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Sterling"} Jonathan Sterling
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+Birkedal"} Lars Birkedal
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Implementing Modal Dependent Type Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://jozefg.github.io/papers/2019-implementing-modal-dependent-type-theory.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://github.com/jozefg/blott"} GitHub
            %li
              %p
                Daniel Gratzer,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Implementing Modal Dependent Type Theory
                %a{:href => "https://icfp19.sigplan.org/"} ICFP 19
                (
                %a{:href => "https://jozefg.github.io/papers/2019-icfp-modal-slides.pdf"}> slides pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Gratzer"}> Daniel Gratzer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G.+Alex+Kavvos"} G. Alex Kavvos
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Nuyts"} Andreas Nuyts
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lars+Birkedal"} Lars Birkedal
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Multimodal Dependent Type Theory
                %strong 17
                3 (2021) lmcs:7713 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.15021"}> arXiv:2011.15021
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.46298/lmcs-17(3:11)2021"} doi:10.46298/lmcs-17(3:11)2021
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                %em Semantics of multimodal adjoint type theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2303.02572"}> arXiv:2303.02572
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Gratzer"}> Daniel Gratzer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Syntax and semantics of modal type theory
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://jozefg.github.io/papers/phd-thesis.pdf"} pdf
          %p A survey of the field of modal type theory is in the collections
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                M. Fairtlough, M. Mendler,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eugenio+Moggi"} Eugenio Moggi
                (eds.),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modalities in Type Theory
            %li#PaivaGoreMendler
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valeria+de+Paiva"}> Valeria de Paiva
                , Rajeev Goré, Michael Mendler,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modalities in constructive logics and type theories
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.cs.bham.ac.uk/~vdp/publications/final-preface.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Valeria+de+Paiva"}> Valeria de Paiva
                , Brigitte Pientka (eds.)
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Intuitionistic Modal Logic and Applications (IMLA 2008)
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S089054011100143X"} web
          %p
            The historically first modal type theory, the interpretation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheafification"} sheafification
            as a modality in the internal language of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            topos goes back to the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lawvere-Tierney+operator"} Lawvere-Tierney operator
          %ul
            %li#Lawvere
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bill+Lawvere"}> Bill Lawvere
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantifiers and sheaves
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://pdfs.semanticscholar.org/6630/846a00261a071b71e264e0f532e29cd9152f.pdf"} pdf
          %p reviewed in
          %ul
            %li#Goldblatt
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Goldblatt"}> Robert Goldblatt
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Grothendieck topology as geometric modality
          %p
            Modal type theory with an eye towards
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UF-IAS-2012"}> UF-IAS-2012
              ,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://uf-ias-2012.wikispaces.com/Modal+type+theory"} Modal type theory
          %p
            Formalization of modalities in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+homotopy+type+theory"}> modal homotopy type theory
            ) is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Shulman
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higher modalities
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UF-IAS-2012"} UF-IAS-2012
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://uf-ias-2012.wikispaces.com/file/view/modalitt.pdf"} pdf
            %li#RSS
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Egbert+Rijke"}> Egbert Rijke
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"} Mike Shulman
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bas+Spitters"} Bas Spitters
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Modalities in homotopy type theory
                %strong 16
                1 (2020) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1706.07526"}> arXiv:1706.07526
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://lmcs.episciences.org/6015"} episciences:6015
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.23638/LMCS-16(1:2)2020"} doi:10.23638/LMCS-16(1:2)2020
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
                ,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "http://homotopytypetheory.org/2015/07/05/modules-for-modalities/"} http://homotopytypetheory.org/2015/07/05/modules-for-modalities/
                (2015)
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Egbert+Rijke"}> Egbert Rijke
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bas+Spitters"} Bas Spitters
                %em Sets in homotopy type theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1305.3835"}> arXiv:1305.3835
                )
            %li
              %p
                Kevin Quirin and Nicolas Tabareau,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Lawvere-Tierney Sheafification in Homotopy Type Theory
                %a{:href => "http://hott-uf.gforge.inria.fr/2015/"} Workshop
                talk 2015 (
                %a{:href => "http://hott-uf.gforge.inria.fr/2015/HOTTUF_Kevin.pdf"}> pdf
                ), Kevin Quirin
                %a{:href => "https://kevinquirin.github.io/thesis/main.pdf"} thesis
          %p
            and specifically in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
